LOCATION:SIDNEY AND LOIS ESKENAZI MUSEUM OF ART
SUMMARY:Curator-led Pop-up Tours
DESCRIPTION:Tours may repeat over the hour on a drop-in basis. Ben Levy w
 ill be available from 5-6 pm in the gallery.&#160\;  About the exhibition
   Spanning more than 150 years\, The Photographic Reflex celebrates a tra
 nsformative gift to the Eskenazi Museum of Art at Indiana University: mor
 e than one hundred works by eighty artists\, generously donated by Lee Ma
 rks and John C. DePrez Jr. This exhibition features photographs dating fr
 om 1856 to 2017 and highlights the medium's extraordinary range\, both as
  a tool for documentation and as a vehicle for artistic exploration.  Pho
 tographs dating from 1856 to 2017 reveal photography's dual role as both 
 a tool for documentation and a vehicle for artistic exploration. Organize
 d into five thematic sections—Play of Light\, Industrial Eye\, Photogra
 phic Figure\, Witness to Conflict\, and Impressed by Nature's Hand—the 
 exhibition examines how photographers across disciplines have communicate
 d ideas\, recorded history\, and shaped visual culture.  The exhibition i
 ncludes works by many of the most influential figures in the medium\, fro
 m Berenice Abbott\, Alfred Stieglitz\, Edward Steichen\, and Henri Cartie
 r-Bresson to contemporary artists such as Edward Burtynsky\, Abelardo Mor
 ell\, Susan Derges\, and Vik Muniz.Read more about this exhibition. (http
 s://artmuseum.indiana.edu/exhibitions/2026-08-29-photographic-reflex-mark
 s-deprez.html)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Tours may repeat over the hour on a dro
 p-in basis. Ben Levy will be available from 5-6 pm in the gallery.&#160\;
 </p><h3>  About the exhibition</h3><p>  Spanning more than 150 years\, <e
 m>The Photographic Reflex</em> celebrates a transformative gift to the Es
 kenazi Museum of Art at Indiana University: more than one hundred works b
 y eighty artists\, generously donated by Lee Marks and John C. DePrez Jr.
  This exhibition features photographs dating from 1856 to 2017 and highli
 ghts the medium's extraordinary range\, both as a tool for documentation 
 and as a vehicle for artistic exploration.</p><p>  Photographs dating fro
 m 1856 to 2017 reveal photography's dual role as both a tool for document
 ation and a vehicle for artistic exploration. Organized into five themati
 c sections—<i>Play of Light</i>\, <i>Industrial Eye</i>\, <i>Photograph
 ic Figure</i>\, <i>Witness to Conflict</i>\, and <i>Impressed by Nature's
  Hand</i>—the exhibition examines how photographers across disciplines 
 have communicated ideas\, recorded history\, and shaped visual culture.</
 p><p>  The exhibition includes works by many of the most influential figu
 res in the medium\, from Berenice Abbott\, Alfred Stieglitz\, Edward Stei
 chen\, and Henri Cartier-Bresson to contemporary artists such as Edward B
 urtynsky\, Abelardo Morell\, Susan Derges\, and Vik Muniz.</p><p><a href=
 "https://artmuseum.indiana.edu/exhibitions/2026-08-29-photographic-reflex
 -marks-deprez.html">Read more about this exhibition.</a></p>
