LOCATION:SIDNEY AND LOIS ESKENAZI MUSEUM OF ART
GEO:39.169144;-86.519739
SUMMARY:Public Tour: Nature-inspired Art
DESCRIPTION:The tour visits three floors of the museum\, looking at a var
 iety of garden-inspired artworks from global cultures. Highlights may inc
 lude:from the forest of Papa New Guinea\, an artwork made from natural ma
 terials\, representing a leaf spirit\, worn for a night dance  a treasure
 d bowl that held kola nuts\, for welcoming guests to an African palace  a
  sacred object from an indigenous culture (Maori)\, made for ocean voyage
 s from a single tree that can live to be 1\,000 years old  a large guard\
 , with ancient origins\, finely crafted\, by a Kenyan artist\, into an ex
 quisite food storage container and later repaired  Hand-crafted bamboo ba
 skets from Japan  Paintings of larkspurs\, chrysanthemums\, and more    P
 lus\, look for signs of nature and discuss the influence of garden experi
 ences on I.M. Pei's iconic architecture.  Each tour may vary slightly\, d
 epending on the tour guide.Know Before You GoAdmission and tours are free
 .Check in with Guest Services at least 5 minutes before the tour start ti
 me\, to claim your reservation. At 1:25 p.m. any unclaimed spaces may be 
 given to guests who are waiting for a space.Read about where to find us\,
  where to park\, accessibility\, and museum guidelines.To save time at th
 e lockers\, avoid bringing backpacks or large bags.  When You ArriveCheck
  in with Guest Services staff on the first floor by 1:25 p.m.Store any ba
 ckpacks or large bags. Lockers and coatrack are available.Do not touch ar
 twork. Be mindful to keep all body parts – and anything you're carrying
  – at least 18" (a half meter) away from artwork.Photography is welcome
 \, without flash or selfie stick.If you parked in the IMU parking lot\, G
 uest Services can validate your parking voucher for a discount.    If thi
 s page says registration has closed\, this means the waitlist is full.  T
 o request this tour at another time for a group of 6-24 people\, contact 
 Laura at ematours@iu.edu (mailto:ematours@iu.edu).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The tour visits three floors of the mus
 eum\, looking at a variety of garden-inspired artworks from global cultur
 es. Highlights may include:</p><ul><li>from the forest of Papa New Guinea
 \, an artwork made from natural materials\, representing a leaf spirit\, 
 worn for a night dance  </li><li>a treasured bowl that held kola nuts\, f
 or welcoming guests to an African palace  </li><li>a sacred object from a
 n indigenous culture (Maori)\, made for ocean voyages from a single tree 
 that can live to be 1\,000 years old  </li><li>a large guard\, with ancie
 nt origins\, finely crafted\, by a Kenyan artist\, into an exquisite food
  storage container and later repaired  </li><li>Hand-crafted bamboo baske
 ts from Japan  </li><li>Paintings of larkspurs\, chrysanthemums\, and mor
 e  </li></ul><p>  Plus\, look for signs of nature and discuss the influen
 ce of garden experiences on I.M. Pei's iconic architecture.</p><p>  Each 
 tour may vary slightly\, depending on the tour guide.</p><p><strong><span
  data-contrast="auto">Know Before You Go</span></strong><span data-ccp-pr
